    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70045</id>
    <title>Balancing coherence and resonance through linguistic framing: A longitudinal study of German biotechnology ventures' responses to the Covid‐19 pandemic</title>
    <updated>2026-09-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Niklas Dreymann, Indre Maurer</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Research suggests that ventures benefit from framing to gain audience support, yet how they utilize framing when exogenous shocks redirect attention remains unclear. Drawing on a longitudinal multiple‐case study of six biotechnology ventures following the COVID‐19 pandemic, we show how ventures balance coherence and resonance through framing pathways. Persevering ventures, committed to pre‐shock opportunities, maintained a “Blockbuster” framing, highlighting business viability to engage commercial investors. Flexible ventures, in contrast, adopted an “Emergency” framing, signaling responsiveness to societal threats to attract public investors, before transitioning to a “Preparedness” framing, emphasizing long‐term potential. Across both pathways, ventures anchored framings in a scientific perspective, signaling expertise and rigor. We theorize how ventures balance coherence and resonance through an anchoring frame that provides continuity and complementing frames that enable adaptation to shifting audience expectations.Managerial SummaryOur study of six biotechnology ventures shows how high‐technology entrepreneurs communicate with investors during industry shocks, such as pandemics. We identify two distinct pathways. Ventures that pivot toward shock‐related opportunities benefit from adopting an “Emergency” framing, positioning themselves as addressing urgent societal threats to attract public investors. To sustain investor support over time, they subsequently shift to a “Preparedness” framing, highlighting their solutions' long‐term potential. Conversely, ventures that maintain their original strategies gain traction by consistently employing a “Blockbuster” framing, emphasizing their business case to attract commercial investors. Across both pathways, ventures reinforce credibility by anchoring their framings in scientific expertise, rigor, and the ability to address unmet medical needs. Pivoting ventures also leverage new applications to identify and capitalize on additional shock‐related opportunities.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70045"/>
    <category term="Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal"/>
    <published>2026-09-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag105</id>
    <title>Machine Learning for Dynamic Incentive Problems</title>
    <updated>2026-09-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Philipp Renner, Simon Scheidegger</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Economic Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We present a flexible and scalable computational framework integrating machine learning and optimization theory to solve dynamic adverse selection models with persistent private information and many types. Our approach reformulates the model into a numerically tractable structure that bypasses set-valued dynamic programming; we formally prove that, under verifiable conditions, this relaxation yields the solution to the original problem. The recast problem is solved via a parallelized value function iteration algorithm, where high-dimensional, nonlinear functions are approximated using Gaussian process regression combined with Bayesian active learning. We apply our framework to two previously intractable models: one with persistent hidden information involving up to ten types and another incorporating multiple persistent types and overreporting. Validation against known solutions and rigorous credibility measures confirms accuracy. Allowing overreporting significantly alters long-run contract outcomes, concentrating consumption away from extremes and smoothing utility promises over time.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag105"/>
    <category term="Review of Economic Studies"/>
    <published>2026-09-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
