    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70034</id>
    <title>Temporal grammars of novelty: How carbon removal entrepreneurs frame new technologies</title>
    <updated>2026-09-21T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Ioana Sendroiu</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper assesses the ways in which carbon dioxide removal ventures frame their work. Drawing on George Herbert Mead's philosophy of the present, it conceptualizes temporal grammars of novelty as a framing strategy that involves connecting two points in time, namely present and future, to elaborate novelty. Dictionary, dependency parsing, and large language model approaches trace the use of temporal grammars of novelty across the websites of carbon removal companies in the market, and connect this to venture sales volumes. Analyses show that ventures deploying newer technologies and using temporal grammars of novelty have higher sales. In contrast, explicitly causal language does not prove beneficial. This paper addresses a fundamental problem in conceptualizing novelty by arguing that it is referential without being causal, and shows this with reference to the framing strategies deployed by environmental entrepreneurs.Managerial SummaryCarbon removal entrepreneurs must convince buyers that novel, often unproven technologies are credible. This paper shows that how they talk about time matters. Analyzing website language and sales across carbon removal ventures, it finds that firms using more speculative technologies sell more when they link present action to future possibility: what they are designing, building, or deploying now, and what this will make possible later, at scale. By contrast, causal language—detailed claims about why or how specific features will produce outcomes—is not helpful and is negatively associated with sales. When framing novelty, pairing present action with future promise is more credible than over‐specified causal certainty.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70034"/>
    <category term="Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal"/>
    <published>2026-09-21T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag107</id>
    <title>Revisiting Retirement and Social Security Claiming Decisions</title>
    <updated>2026-09-21T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Neha Bairoliya, Kathleen McKiernan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Economic Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;The structure of the Social Security program presents a trade-off between benefit duration and size; individuals who claim early receive lower monthly payments. Surprisingly, even wealthy Americans with high life expectancies frequently claim benefits at the earliest eligibility age, a behavior inconsistent with conventional life-cycle theory predictions. In fact, there is a striking similarity in claiming ages across groups with vastly different life expectancies, wealth levels, and incentives to delay. This paper resolves this puzzle by introducing policy details and subjective beliefs about longevity and program rules into a rich, forward-looking life-cycle model. When included in the model, subjective beliefs substantially narrow the claiming-age gaps across education (college vs. non-college) and marital-status (married vs. single) groups. Specifically, differences in program beliefs alone account for 48.6% of the education claiming age gap and 75.1% of the marital status gap. Counterfactual policy simulations demonstrate that failing to account for these claiming mechanisms dramatically overstates individual responses to an increase in the normal retirement age and understates the resulting fiscal savings.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag107"/>
    <category term="Review of Economic Studies"/>
    <published>2026-09-21T00:00:00+00:00</published>
