    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2023.0246</id>
    <title>When “Signals” Boomerang: Employers’ Reactions to a Novel Signaling Mechanism</title>
    <updated>2026-09-22T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Qiang Gao, Mingfeng Lin, Yong Liu, Chenhui Guo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Online labor platforms often introduce new features to help employers identify reliable workers. This study examines one such feature: a worker-offered guarantee, in which freelancers voluntarily deposit their own money with a bid, with forfeited funds donated to charity rather than paid to the employer. Although such guarantees are intended to signal confidence and commitment, our analysis of bid-level data from a major freelance platform shows that employers interpret them differently. Workers with medium reputations are most likely to offer guarantees, and employers are less likely to hire workers who use them. This skepticism appears economically justified: guarantee-offering workers receive lower post-contract ratings on average. The findings suggest that well-intentioned platform tools can backfire when users see them as signs of weakness or desperation rather than quality. For platform managers and policymakers interested in digital labor markets, the study highlights the need to design signals with credible costs, clear enforcement, and alignment with existing reputation systems. New marketplace features should be evaluated not only by whether users adopt them, but also by how other market participants interpret and act on them.</content>
