    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2025.2166</id>
    <title>Impact of the Invisibles: Personalized Pricing on Platform with Privacy-Preserving Users</title>
    <updated>2026-09-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jieqiong (Julie) Wang, Zhaohui (Zoey) Jiang, Param Vir Singh</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;What happens when consumers refuse to share their data? They may end up paying more. This study finds that when some shoppers protect their privacy, e-commerce platforms can strategically group them with other consumers in ways that make sellers less aggressive in competing for their business. The result is surprising; both the platform and third-party sellers can charge higher prices, whereas privacy-preserving consumers—and even some consumers who share their data—can be worse off.The broader lesson is that privacy changes more than what firms know about consumers. It can change how firms compete. When information becomes less complete, platforms may find new ways to soften competition and increase profits. For consumers and policymakers, that means the effects of privacy rules cannot be judged only by asking whether firms have less access to personal data. We also need to ask what firms do differently once that information is no longer available.</content>
